#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""How responsive a turn is, per backend, measured on a disposable Host. Run by hand, never a gate.

    server-rs/tools/turn-timings run    [--backends codex,claude,agy] [--codex-lane] [--claude-lane] [--rounds N] [--cases LIST]
                                        [--bin DIR] [--out DIR] [--model ROW=MODEL ...]
                                        [--effort ROW=EFFORT ...] [--keep-host]
                                        [--lane-idle-minutes N] [--lane-most N] [--lane-memory-mb N] [--lane-settle SECONDS]
    server-rs/tools/turn-timings report RUN_DIR

`run` starts a Host of its own (its own data, port, token and an empty configuration folder),
makes one test agent per backend on a cheap model at low effort, and runs every case `--rounds`
times (default 5). The cases, and what each measures:

  plain   "reply with one word". Send to the first live activity of the turn (time to first
          activity), send to the turn's end (end to end), and the launch overhead split where the
          spool lets it: the Host's queue (send to the shim's spool being made), the launch (spool
          made to the tool's process up, the shim's `spawned`) and the tool's own start (process
          up to its first line of output).
  tool    "run `ls`, then reply". The tool call's round trip as the Host shows it: the activity
          that says the command runs to the one that says it finished (same call id), and the
          same span in the spool, from the tool's own lines.
  steer   "run `sleep 20`, then reply"; while the command runs a second message is sent. Steer to
          effect: the second send to its row in the conversation (`GET /log`). Whether the
          running turn took it (steered) or it waited for the next turn (queued) is recorded,
          and for a queued one how long it waited until its own turn started.
  stop    "run `sleep 30`, then reply"; Stop while the command runs. Stop to the Host saying the
          turn is over, and Stop to the tool's process being gone (the spool's `exited`, and the
          process id no longer there).

  queued  (only when named in `--cases`) a first turn that writes a 50-line list, and a second
          message sent two seconds in with `queue_if_busy`, so that it waits on every backend.
          From the first turn's last line to the second turn's first activity, step by step:
          the tool's process gone (`exited`), the Host settling the turn, the Host starting the
          next one, its spool made, `spawned`, the tool's first line, the model's first reply
          line and the Host's first activity of it; and to the second turn's end.

`--claude-lane` adds the `claude-lane` row: Claude with `claude_chat_lane` on. Its first turn runs
in a process of its own, as every turn does without a warm lane, and the agent's kept Claude Code is
started once that process is gone; the row's warm-up waits for it. `--port` gives the disposable Host
a port of its own choosing instead of any free one.

`--codex-lane` adds the `codex-lane` row (as does naming it in `--backends`). It is Codex with the
Host's `codex_chat_lane` setting on (written to the
disposable Host's own config.toml, read at each turn), so its chat turns run on the agent's
long-lived app-server lane. It makes one unmeasured warm-up turn first, so that its cases measure
a warm lane, and adds a case:

  cold    a plain turn of a new agent each round: its first turn, which starts the lane and its
          thread. Every turn of the row says whether it started a lane (`lane_new`), and the
          summary shows such turns apart from the warm ones.

On that row the steer case also measures the second send to the lane's `steer.read` line in the
spool and to Codex's first item after it, and the stop case allows the app-server to outlive the
turn (`process_kept`). After its cases it reads the memory of every idle lane: the
`clarp-shim codex-goal-lane`, its app-server child and that child's process group (where an MCP
server the owner's Codex configuration starts lives), RSS and PSS, twice, `--lane-settle` seconds
apart (default 30), with the `idle_ms` the lane's Inspect answers. On a Host without chat lanes
the row runs on exec and the summary says no lane was seen.

Every case records the machine's load (os.getloadavg and `uptime`) before and after it, because
this machine is busy: never compare runs taken at very different loads. The raw timings go to
`<out>/<run-id>/timings.json` and a summary (median and p90 per measure, per backend) to
`summary.md` beside it. `report` writes the summary again from a `timings.json`.

The disposable Host follows hard rule 2 of server-rs/REBUILD.md: its own `--data`, port and
token; XDG_CONFIG_HOME and CLARP_CONFIG_DIR an empty folder of its own, so it never reads the
real peers.json and never reaches another Host; no relay, push keys or push relay; the direct
launcher. `GET /peers` must answer an empty list before the first case, or the run stops. The
binaries are the installed release (~/.local/share/clarp-host/bin) unless `--bin` names a dist
folder; nothing is built. The tools run on the machine's own logins as they are: nothing logs in
or out, and nothing is copied. The run stops its Host by the process id it started and deletes
the Host's folders (unless `--keep-host`). Claude test agents run on Sonnet (hard rule 8).

Claude Code and muse keep their login under a folder the run gives them (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, and
XDG_CONFIG_HOME for muse): the run's own folder holds only a link to each live login file, so
what else they write stays with the run. After the run `login_links` says whether a link is
still a link (a tool that renewed its login may have written a file in its place; nothing is
ever copied).

Before and after the run it looks at the tools' own settings files for an entry naming the run's
folder (a trust entry, a project), removes one if a tool wrote it, and says so in the summary.
Nothing here prints conversation text: the spool is read for record types and times only.
"""
